enclave SET epochs opt-out: -W set-epochs=n removes SET's last engine tax
=========================================================================

THE COST (measured 2026-08-22, doom fb machine, local minio rig, guest MIPS
as /status instret delta over 20s, same-revision guest builds, engine at
this patch stack): declaring shared-everything-threads costs a tenant ~16%
of its hot-loop throughput, and NONE of it is the shared memory itself:

    plain build, plain flags           122.4 / 127.9 MIPS
    plain build, epoch codegen on      110.2 / 114.5   (--wasm timeout)
    SET build, SET flags (today)       105.8 / 107.5
    SET build, SET flags, set-epochs=n         117.9   (this patch)

Bounds checks were the SUSPECT, and they are INNOCENT: wasmtime 49 already
elides them for shared memories (the elision branch in
crates/cranelift/src/bounds_checks.rs and Memory::can_elide_bounds_check
never test `shared`; verified by objdump on a shared/unshared minimal pair
and on risc-box-set's Mmu hot functions - load sites are direct guard-page
accesses either way). The measured tax is the EPOCH INTERRUPTION the SET
patch forces on (a check at every function entry and loop back-edge:
27-40% on a bare memory loop, ~10% on risc-box's interpreter), plus a ~6%
residual attributable to the SET guest toolchain, not the engine.

THE OPT-OUT: `-W set-epochs=n` (default y keeps today's behavior exactly).
With it, run/serve skip the epoch-interruption Config bit, the
SetEpochTicker, and the per-store SET epoch callbacks. What still works,
and why this is a PROMPTNESS trade rather than a soundness one (module
header of vm/component/set_threads.rs is the contract):

  - a worker parked in memory.atomic.wait stops instantly (parking-spot
    group stop flag; probe: set-park-teardown, 9ms clean exit);
  - a worker blocked in a host call stops (its guest future is dropped);
  - teardown of a worker SPINNING in pure compute is still bounded: join
    times out (ENCLAVE_SET_JOIN_TIMEOUT_MS, default 2s) and the reaper
    DETACHES it with the existing loud diagnostic - sound because workers
    hold no raw pointers into the primary store (probe: set-spin-teardown,
    2.0s exit with the detach warning; with epochs it is 20ms clean);
  - `--wasm timeout` OVERRIDES the opt-out (its worker enforcement runs
    through the epoch deadline callback), so set-epochs=n can never
    silently weaken a requested timeout: verified, the timeout run stays
    at epoch-armed speed and traps on schedule.

What is LOST: prompt interruption of pure-compute spinners (they burn CPU
inside the tenant's cgroup weight until process exit - the platform's stop
is SIGKILL anyway) and prompt cross-thread proc_exit propagation to a
spinning primary (delayed to its next park or host call). Under `serve`
the detach-per-request shape can accumulate; the process-global live-thread
cap bounds it (further spawns refuse into the sequential fallback). The
one-time "engine WITHOUT Config::epoch_interruption" stderr warning from
spawn still fires: the operator chose this, but the log should say so.

Wired per-tenant by wasm_manager._set_flags (probed, so a new manager on an
old engine omits the flag instead of failing every SET launch).

tests/all after: 1305 passed / 0 failed - identical to the SET promotion
baseline. Applies LAST, after wasmtime-socket-level-check (diffed against
the fully-patched tree).

