+/// enclave/SET: whether this SET run arms the epoch machinery — interruption
+/// codegen on the engine plus the periodic ticker.
+///
+/// On by default. `-W set-epochs=n` is the deliberate opt-out for embedders
+/// that accept process supervision as the stop of last resort: the epoch
+/// checks SET otherwise compiles into every function entry and loop back-edge
+/// are the whole compile-time cost of declaring SET (measured ~10% guest MIPS
+/// on risc-box's interpreter loop, 27-40% on a bare memory loop). Without
+/// them a worker parked in `memory.atomic.wait` still stops (parking-spot
+/// wake), a worker blocked in a host call still stops (its guest future is
+/// dropped), and teardown still terminates (bounded join, then the reaper
+/// detaches with a diagnostic — sound because workers hold no raw pointers
+/// into the primary store). What is LOST is prompt interruption of a worker
+/// spinning in pure compute, and prompt `proc_exit` propagation to a primary
+/// spinning in pure compute; both then wait for the next park/host call or
+/// for process exit.
+///
+/// `--wasm timeout` overrides the opt-out rather than silently weakening:
+/// its enforcement on workers runs through the epoch deadline callback, so a
+/// requested timeout re-arms the full machinery.
+pub fn set_epochs_enabled(common: &CommonOptions) -> bool {
+    set_threads_enabled(common)
+        && (common.wasm.set_epochs.unwrap_or(true) || common.wasm.timeout.is_some())
+}
